VGE-owned Simple and Volumetric clouds
=====================================

Authority and scope
  Source: docs/PBR.VolumetricClouds.Proposal.md (approved 2026-10-16).
  Parent: docs/PBR.BaselineShading.todo, physically motivated volumetric clouds.
  Atmospheric contract: docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md.
  This is a planning-only execution checklist. No implementation or validation is claimed here.
  The proposal governs scope; keep every task unchecked until its stated evidence exists.
  Preserve Off / Simple / Volumetric settings and both standalone PBR and PBR + LumOn operation.
  Retain native weather, geometry and column-map production; VGE owns visible shaders and lighting.
  Preserve cubic shapes, native column bounds, existing OIT and the frozen scene-lighting scale.
  Cast shadows onto scene/atmosphere are deferred to virtual shadow maps. Do not implement Beer
  shadow maps, earlier map generation or previous-frame shadow publication for those consumers.
  Do not implement the separate transparency proposal, replace weather simulation, generate a
  complete cloud-density volume, alter installed shaders, or optimize away native map resources.

Execution conventions
  Use domain-focused files with thin composition roots and directional dependencies. Give every
  shader its dedicated VGE program class and existing shader-build/registration contract.
  Follow repository comments, visibility/region and resource-ownership conventions.
  Run build/test tools through subagents. Keep ordinary correctness checks lightweight; full-size
  reference captures and GPU timing are explicit qualification activities. Never launch the game
  for visual acceptance; the user performs that acceptance.
  Phase completion means its increment is verified, not that the entire feature is complete.
  If a required existing integration is unavailable, record the dependency; do not silently add
  an alternative renderer, weather producer, transparency system or shadow pipeline.

Implementation parameters to establish
  The native source evidence is from 1.22.7; verify installed contracts before relying on it.
  Exact sample counts, coefficients, noise implementation, rejection thresholds and resource formats
  are bounded implementation choices. They must satisfy the approved contracts, not reopen them.
  Target hardware, resolution and performance budgets are not specified. Record explicit targets
  before performance acceptance; absent targets leave that acceptance open without blocking math work.
  Reflection/environment consumer wiring and map-readiness detection require source verification.

Phase 1: Verify inputs and define rendering contracts
  Outcome: subsequent work has concrete native-data and VGE-consumer contracts.
  ☐ Reverify native producers, callbacks and resource lifetimes against the installed version.
    - Record native map OIT 0.30, volumetric draw 0.31, Simple callback 0.35, settings watchers,
      LevelFinalize creation, pause/time-speed behavior, reload and teardown ownership.
    - Identify observable first tile upload and successful map-generation readiness; allocated
      textures alone are insufficient. Verify rebuild detection and current placement metadata.
    - Verify Simple's 12-short/24-byte instance layout, vertex inputs and post-CloudTick mesh access.
    - Confirm map.r extinction semantics, map.ba units and the installed hardcoded 50-world-unit scale.
  ☐ Specify VGE input/output contracts using existing owners.
    - Define explicit metres-per-world-unit conversion, world/generation identity and borrowed-resource
      validity. No worker Tiles reads, CPU map readback or copy for immediate same-frame drawing.
    - Trace physical atmosphere, local fog, scene storage admission, liquid depth, OIT buffers/resolve,
      environmental and reflection consumers; record where each attenuation/encoding occurs once.
    - Define bounded HDR output admission before OIT draws and identify conditional native compatibility
      allowances/patches that must remain for restored native paths and the retained cloud-map producer.
    - Specify OIT-compatible intermediate payload for temporal reconstruction, preserving bucket
      weighting/revealage rather than reducing the cloud to one depthless overlay.
    - Define reflection camera/depth handling through existing consumers; no main-view history reuse.
  ☐ Record baseline validation commands and representative scene matrix for both PBR modes.
    - Establish current focused build/test baseline through a subagent; distinguish pre-existing failures.
    - Record target hardware/resolution/budgets when available, without inventing acceptance numbers.
  ☐ Completion: source receipts and input/output contracts cover both modes and their consumers;
    readiness and ownership hooks are concrete, and unsupported dependencies are explicitly recorded.

Phase 2: Implement native adapters and owned resource boundaries
  Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: coherent cloud inputs are available without changing weather ownership.
  ☐ Implement narrow adapters for native map and Simple owners.
    - Acquire instances after creation. Borrow completed map plus current dimensions/offsets after
      generation; invalidate on failed generation, rebuild, reload or world change and omit unavailable
      cloud contributions. Never assume an in-place failed update retained valid contents.
    - Expose current Simple mesh/count/committed placement only after its update; do not cache meshes
      across callbacks, dispose them, or replace the native shader field.
    - Keep producer pause behavior and native shader subscriptions intact.
  ☐ Establish VGE lifecycle, shader registration and resource owners.
    - Separate mode ownership, borrowed-input adaptation, density/lighting, draw and history concerns.
    - Use existing GPU abstractions, settings/demand loading and shader build contracts; document units
      and ownership in APIs. Resource creation/replacement follows lifecycle changes, not every frame.
    - Prepare activation/restoration paths but activate each replacement only with its functioning
      draw consumer in the following phases; do not leave native drawing withdrawn by unfinished work.
  ☐ Verify adapters with focused readiness/replacement/identity tests using deterministic inputs.
    - Cover allocated-but-uninitialized input, failed generation, changed handles/dimensions, pause,
      stale-world references and ownership of disposal. No test should require launching the game.
  ☐ Completion: adapters reject unavailable/stale inputs, preserve native ownership, and do not alter
    visible mode behavior before a corresponding VGE draw is ready.

Phase 3: Deliver VGE Simple rendering
  Depends on Phase 2. Outcome: Simple uses the native mesh and VGE shaders at the native draw position.
  ☐ Implement VGE Simple vertex/fragment programs consuming the existing layout directly.
    - Preserve cube/face flags, normalized offsets rescaled by 32767, neighboring thickness, own
      thickness, thin/undulating modes and opacity; no mesh copy or full volume/noise evaluation.
    - Preserve height, placement, curvature, undulation and shared-face coverage; supply current
      projection/model-view, tile offset, cloud extent/altitude and animation inputs.
    - Supply perception intensity locally with the native factor 20; never mutate engine uniforms.
    - Treat legacy brightness/base color as artistic metadata, not physical incident illumination.
  ☐ Bind completed VGE atmosphere and scene-color contracts.
    - Replace native daylight/sunset/fog coloration; apply existing fog once. Preserve adjusted OIT
      depth, coverage and liquid-depth interaction. Map glow metadata to the existing VGE output owner.
  ☐ Intercept CloudRenderer.OnRenderFrame at OIT 0.35 only while VGE owns Simple.
    - Preserve mode gating; call native CloudTick once when unpaused, then obtain and draw the current
      mesh and suppress the original body. Preserve the zero-width draw guard and paused camera updates.
    - Relinquishing ownership lets the original callback run; retain native mesh/shader reload owners.
    - Reconcile Simple native compatibility bounds/patch selection without breaking disable restoration.
  ☐ Verify attribute decoding, geometry/coverage controls, local perception and finite HDR/OIT outputs.
    - Exercise exactly-once tick/draw gating, paused draws and post-tick mesh replacement; run focused
      shader compilation and small headless scenes through a subagent.
  ☐ Completion: focused evidence establishes the Simple contract and restoration behavior; live
    appearance/performance remain explicitly pending final acceptance.

Phase 4: Deliver full-resolution volumetric reference transport
  Depends on Phase 2 and the shared integration contracts exercised in Phase 3.
  Outcome: a history-free reference implements the complete density, lighting and OIT model.
  ☐ Implement bounded column intersection and density evaluation.
    - Use native TextureMap.r and .ba, tile footprints, altitude and placement; never use TextureCol.
    - Use sigmaBase = p / (50 * metresPerWorldUnit) and T = exp(-sigmaT * dsMetres).
      Do not clamp p to one, reinterpret it as alpha, or duplicate native fading/perception factors.
    - Reject negative/nonfinite inputs. Reconcile any producer-unit change explicitly.
    - Evaluate detail = (1 + a*(2*n-1))*edge for n in [0,1], 0 <= a < 1 and edge in [0,1].
      Provide detail=1 reference; keep zero native extinction zero and density outside columns zero.
    - Soften exposed boundaries inward, not occupied shared faces. Keep broad faces/steps, clear regions
      and connected clouds; no rounded replacement envelope or automatic density compensation.
    - Anchor noise across tiles/recentering and native wind without double advection. Bound addressing
      to available map data; no stretched border, abrupt added cutoff or invented missing weather.
  ☐ Implement view and light transport using one shared density definition.
    - Clip scene/liquid depths, integrate from camera inside clouds, handle above/below/horizontal rays,
      skip only provably empty regions and terminate saturated rays with bounded sample work.
    - Use completed solar/lunar/environment inputs and bounded secondary marches for self-shadowing.
      Add exactly one approximate scattering octave beyond single scattering; tune finite coefficients.
    - Apply atmosphere/local fog and storage encoding once; include physical output in frozen range
      admission. Preserve existing cloud OIT depth-dependent weighting and revealage.
  ☐ Register the VGE reference draw at OIT 0.31 with native map generation retained at 0.30.
    - Never independently tick the map. Withdraw native volumetric drawing and keep it withdrawn after
      native settings watchers re-register it. Restore current-setting behavior when VGE is disabled.
    - Activate only the selected mode; preserve Off and Simple without forcing preferences.
    - Integrate environmental/reflection consumers using Phase 1 contracts and their own camera/depth
      inputs, without introducing deferred cast shadows or a second atmosphere integrator.
    - Reconcile native volumetric compatibility paths while preserving restored native drawing and
      the still-running native map/color pass and its legacy resource requirements.
  ☐ Verify transport with analytic and small headless fixtures.
    - No-detail constant columns match native transmittance; include p=2 over 50 units => exp(-2),
      path subdivision, different world-unit conversions, p>1 and empty/invalid columns.
    - Check connected-column edges, outside-volume zero density, stable advection and bounded noise.
    - Verify view/light density agreement, single-plus-one-octave behavior, no duplicate attenuation,
      OIT coverage/buckets, scene/liquid clipping, and both PBR modes without LumOn assumptions.
  ☐ Completion: full-resolution history-free transport is functional and numerically verified;
    it remains a reference mode, not an exact/converged lighting claim or the final default.

Phase 5: Deliver half-resolution temporal rendering
  Depends on Phase 4. Outcome: the approved default reconstructs the same OIT transport contract.
  ☐ Trace at half view width and height with bounded size/format allocation.
    - Preserve required bucket/revealage and depth metadata, finite HDR radiance and dimensionless
      attenuation. Do not reconstruct only a flattened color layer.
  ☐ Implement temporal reconstruction and depth-aware full-resolution upsampling.
    - Account for camera and native cloud motion; distinguish recentering from actual advection.
    - Reject/bound incompatible history for disocclusion, weather/lighting transitions, grid replacement,
      resize, reload, mode/enable changes, unavailable input and world changes.
    - Do not use texture-handle equality as history validity or reuse main-view history in reflections.
    - Preserve foreground silhouettes, liquid clipping, celestial occlusion and OIT composition.
  ☐ Expose the approved default and full-resolution history-free reference using existing settings/tools.
    - Both use identical density/lighting/OIT semantics; reference bypasses reduced-resolution and
      temporal operations. Keep Simple outside this path. Bound sample/detail controls.
  ☐ Compare deterministic small scenes against the reference and verify history reset/reprojection.
    - Cover static convergence, motion, newly exposed geometry, thin columns, weather/light changes,
      mixed-depth transparency, resize and missing input; record precision tolerances and residuals.
  ☐ Completion: half-resolution temporal rendering is the default and reference comparison is
    available; correctness receipts distinguish temporal approximations from base transport errors.

Phase 6: Qualify lifecycle and complete integration
  Depends on Phases 3–5. Outcome: the combined feature preserves settings, ownership and scene contracts.
  ☐ Verify the full Off/Simple/Volumetric and VGE enable/disable transition matrix.
    - Cover repeated mode toggles, native watcher re-registration, current-setting restoration,
      pause/time-speed changes, view-distance rebuild, shader reload success/failure and world re-entry.
    - Establish exactly one visible draw and one selected producer-update path; verify no stale handles,
      double ticks, native resource disposal, orphaned VGE resources or persistent preference changes.
  ☐ Qualify scene integration in both PBR modes.
    - Check sun/moon/stars/sky attenuation, terrain occlusion, particles/translucency/water before,
      within and behind clouds, local fog/underwater treatment, environmental response and reflections.
    - Verify no double fog/weather dimming, no display-color inputs in physical shading and matching
      frame storage/range admission. Existing OIT approximation remains accepted.
  ☐ Finish compatibility cleanup and permanent documentation.
    - Update SceneColorLegacyPatches, SceneColorCloudInputs and compatibility publication only as
      required by owned versus restored paths; retain still-used native map lookup/resource support.
    - Update focused shader-stage/legacy-patch tests to assert real selection and restoration contracts.
    - Remove temporary implementation scaffolding; preserve deliberate native producer dependencies.
    - Document current runtime architecture, units, modes, lifecycle and debug/reference controls;
      reconcile atmosphere publication docs without turning permanent docs into a historical log.
  ☐ Run required build, focused CPU/GPU and relevant integration/package checks through subagents.
    - Verify shader catalog/packaging includes owned programs and no installed game asset modification.
    - Record versions, commands and results; keep source/headless/package receipts distinct from live QA.
  ☐ Completion: required automated and packaging checks pass, docs match final behavior and lifecycle
    evidence supports both modes. Human visual and measured performance gates remain below.

Phase 7: Visual and performance acceptance
  Depends on Phase 6. Outcome: the approved appearance and measured cost are qualified on explicit targets.
  ☐ Prepare bounded reference scenes and user-run visual instructions.
    - Cover clear/thin/thick/overcast weather; moving sun/moon; ground/in-cloud/above-cloud views;
      grid recentering; edges/seams; fog/water/reflections/translucency; and both PBR modes.
    - Compare restrained fluff to detail-disabled native-transmittance and full-resolution history-free
      references. Confirm cubic masses, broad faces/steps and preserved native volume/weather intent.
    - Confirm Simple retains block-shaped geometry and lower-cost rendering without temporal effects.
  ☐ Obtain and record user visual acceptance, including motion/disocclusion and lifecycle transitions.
    - Leave this unchecked while acceptance is missing or issues remain; headless checks are not a substitute.
  ☐ Measure GPU/CPU and memory cost on recorded hardware/resolution/settings and matched scenes.
    - Separate native producer/uploads, Simple, volumetric view/light tracing, reconstruction/upsampling
      and integration costs. Include weather/noise, unused native color output, trace targets and history.
    - Distinguish resident storage from scratch/transient reservations; compare default/reference modes.
    - Check declared budgets; retain performance acceptance as pending if targets or measurements are absent.
  ☐ Reconcile every proposal requirement with implementation and evidence, and review the final result.
    - Record remaining defects/dependencies honestly; deferred virtual-shadow-map work stays deferred.
  ☐ Completion: visual acceptance, measured budget qualification and final review are recorded;
    no required acceptance gate is substituted by an automated or source-only receipt.

Final acceptance
  ☐ All preceding phase criteria and required checks are satisfied with linked evidence.
  ☐ Approved scope is implemented, docs are reconciled and runtime/visual/performance limits are explicit.
  ☐ Update the parent cloud task only after its required acceptance is satisfied; retain the separate
    virtual-shadow-map task and cast-cloud-shadow deferral. Do not mark deferred shadows implemented.

Proposal coverage map
  Ownership and lifecycle: Phases 1–3, 4 activation, 6.
  Simple shaders/geometry: Phase 3; scene/lifecycle qualification: Phases 6–7.
  Native bounds, extinction, cubic fluff and finite domain: Phase 4; visual qualification: Phase 7.
  Atmosphere, scattering, self-shadowing and OIT: Phases 1, 3–4, 6.
  Half-resolution/history-free modes and history: Phases 4–5, 7.
  Environment/reflection consumers: Phases 1, 4, 6–7.
  Evidence/acceptance and measured costs: all phase criteria, Phases 6–7 and Final acceptance.
  Cast shadows, transparency redesign and native-producer redesign: excluded in Authority and scope.
